From: Andrey Bodrov <ap.bodrov@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] linuxkpi: zero the net_device driver private area

linuxkpi_alloc_netdev() allocates sizeof(*ndev) + len and then calls
linuxkpi_init_dummy_netdev(), which memsets only sizeof(*ndev).  The
trailing len bytes -- exactly what netdev_priv() hands back to the driver
-- are never initialised, so every driver private struct starts as
whatever was last in that heap memory.

Linux allocates the whole thing with kvzalloc() in alloc_netdev_mqs() and
drivers depend on it: a pointer field that is only assigned on some paths
is expected to read back NULL otherwise.

The failure this was found through: brcmfmac's struct brcmf_if has
fws_desc, assigned only when brcmf_fws_add_interface() runs to completion.
brcmf_fws_reset_interface() checks it for NULL and then writes through it.
With garbage there (recycled memory that happened to contain instruction
bytes) the NULL check passed and the store faulted, panicking the kernel
as soon as firmware events began to be delivered.

Allocate with M_ZERO.

Signed-off-by: Andrey Bodrov <ap.bodrov@gmail.com>
---
--- a/sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/src/linux_netdev.c
+++ b/sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/src/linux_netdev.c
@@ -398,7 +398,14 @@
 {
 	struct net_device *ndev;
 
-	ndev = malloc(sizeof(*ndev) + len, M_NETDEV, M_NOWAIT);
+	/*
+	 * M_ZERO: the driver's private area lives in the trailing
+	 * drv_priv[len] bytes and linuxkpi_init_dummy_netdev() below only
+	 * memsets sizeof(*ndev), so without this the private struct starts
+	 * out as recycled heap garbage.  Linux allocates the whole thing
+	 * with kvzalloc() and drivers rely on that.
+	 */
+	ndev = malloc(sizeof(*ndev) + len, M_NETDEV, M_NOWAIT | M_ZERO);
 	if (ndev == NULL)
 		return (ndev);
 
